    <h1 class="page-title">Master & Slave (Agent) Architecture</h1>

    <div class="note-card">
      
        <h2>Why Master-Slave?</h2>
        <p>Jenkins uses a Master-Slave (Master-Agent) architecture to manage distributed builds. The Master is the main Jenkins server; Slaves are additional servers/agents that share the workload. Master and Slave nodes communicate over the <strong>TCP/IP</strong> protocol.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Using slaves, jobs get distributed and the load on the master reduces &mdash; Jenkins can run more builds concurrently.</li>
            <li>Allows different environments to be set up on different slaves (Java, .NET, Terraform, etc.).</li>
            <li>Supported slave types: Linux, Windows, Docker, Kubernetes, ECS (AWS).</li>
            <li>If no slaves exist, the master does all the work by default.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Setting Up Master &amp; Slave</h2>
        <ol>
            <li>Launch 3 EC2 instances with the <em>same</em> key-pair (name them master, slave-1, slave-2 for clarity).</li>
            <li>Install Jenkins on the master server, and only Java on the slave servers &mdash; using the <em>same Java version</em> as the master.</li>
            <li>On the master: <strong>Manage Jenkins &rarr; Nodes &amp; Clouds &rarr; New Node</strong>.</li>
            <li>Give the node a name and select <strong>Permanent Agent &rarr; Create</strong>.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>New Node Configuration Fields</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Number of executors</strong> &mdash; default is 2, maximum 5. More executors = faster/parallel builds.</li>
            <li><strong>Remote root directory</strong> &mdash; the slave server path where Jenkins info is stored (e.g. <code>/home/ec2-user</code>).</li>
            <li><strong>Labels</strong> &mdash; a way to tag/name one or more slaves (e.g. "dev", "prod", "linux", "docker").</li>
            <li><strong>Usage</strong> &mdash; select "Only build jobs with label expressions matching this node" so the label is respected.</li>
            <li><strong>Launch method</strong> &mdash; "Launch agents via SSH".</li>
            <li><strong>Host</strong> &mdash; the slave server's public IP address.</li>
            <li><strong>Credentials</strong> &mdash; Kind: SSH Username with private key; add the slave's key-pair PEM content directly.</li>
            <li><strong>Host Key Verification Strategy</strong> &mdash; select "Non verifying Verification Strategy".</li>
            <li><strong>Availability</strong> &mdash; "Keep this agent online as much as possible".</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Note:</strong> If Build Executor status shows an error, it is usually a Java version mismatch &mdash; install the same Java version on the slave that's on the master.</p>

        <h2>Running a Job on a Slave</h2>
        <p>Create a job &rarr; Configure &rarr; check <strong>"Restrict where this project can be run"</strong> &rarr; enter the Label (e.g. <code>Linux</code>) &rarr; add an Execute Shell build step &rarr; Save &amp; Build. The job output will be found on the <em>slave</em> server, not the master &mdash; because the slave is working on behalf of the master.</p>
        <p><strong>Important:</strong> If the Label option is left blank inside a job, it always runs on the master.</p>
        
    </div>

